#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549130} :activity/id #uuid "69edc7b5-e99c-51bc-925d-b4f0cb17b8a1" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050188 :topic/id #uuid "34eb87b9-7036-55c0-88eb-1b1efbd11584" :topic/math-academy-id 3178} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549078 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281496 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f08a63d-f009-56f8-a5cb-f93aa6912138" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278727} {:answer/id #uuid "38426b1c-d8d7-59bd-9f52-75631306e62b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279083} {:answer/id #uuid "460dc314-e0f8-567c-bd40-3099a01fa162" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279218} {:answer/id #uuid "acaf9800-50fa-5703-aed0-9566eece1058" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280121} {:answer/id #uuid "b2c249e2-86e9-57c9-8be7-8978b6d94fdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280170}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280121} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4e7ecce-f0f7-5490-a4b9-42cedda6d7df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281269}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b4054066-10f2-5204-8707-fb86a2d9ed7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-162551" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨3x,\\,2z,\\,y-2⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=2x-y+1$ for $0\\le x\\le 4$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the *downward*-pointing unit normal vector $n.$\n\n*Hint: You're given that the fundamental vector product of $S$ with respect to its canonical parametrization is $||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||=\\sqrt{6}.$*" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=2x-y+1$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-2,\\,1,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=1,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince we require a downward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is negative. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2x-y+1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨3x,\\,2z,\\,y-2⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨3x,\\,4x-2y+2,\\,y-2⟩\\cdot ⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(6x-(4x-2y+2)-(y-2)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(2x+y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(2x+y)\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(2x+y)\\cdot \\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{6}}}(2x+y)\\cdot \\cancel{\\sqrt{6}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4}(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{x}^{2}+xy]}_{x=0}^{x=4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4y+16)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[2{y}^{2}+16y]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0d825271-0805-5607-9fc6-a19d41bb2583" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549109}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549080 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370853 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3fe33c2d-28d8-5c63-af57-2cfea02124a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370855} {:answer/id #uuid "b144a61b-6449-5a95-93b2-5ecb54d3aff5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370856} {:answer/id #uuid "c39ce534-8261-5bb2-a3a7-9565f1b35ea6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370857} {:answer/id #uuid "f6599dfa-b736-51b0-a238-88ebd55fb5ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370858} {:answer/id #uuid "da68bb0d-4fa0-572e-849a-ab56c69bc8e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370859}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370855} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b73cc72-c77c-588f-8710-d900af9a9255" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3c5b1199-265f-51a1-b6b8-07c045672fdb" :question/math-academy-id "q-365884" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\,z,\\,4xy⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=2$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 3.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the *downward*-pointing unit normal vector $n.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is a continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor the surface $z=2$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 3,$ the downward-pointing unit normal vector is\n\n$$\nn=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z,\\,z,\\,4xy⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}(-4xy)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-4xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(-4xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}2y\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{0}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{[{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{3}\\cdot {[{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-36\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1271c0a8-c668-5d48-a3f0-44627558c204"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549085 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281494 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28d556ba-9643-5484-9436-c1e3ecf8ad60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278955} {:answer/id #uuid "7c669bc8-22ad-586a-8953-01b40010091b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279702} {:answer/id #uuid "9007a807-e069-5a10-87d9-4a4fe1f0c21d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279875} {:answer/id #uuid "be726de3-b925-54e4-8808-5f989e45f0ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280265} {:answer/id #uuid "ea59137e-6284-5d86-9d60-6242ac410b1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280650}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279875} :answer-field/id #uuid "7411192a-5fa7-5ba5-bdbc-118ec1d69e3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281043}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "39723672-6400-50eb-bf48-dba5cb75604c" :question/math-academy-id "q-162546" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨y,\\,x,\\,z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=3x-y$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the *downward*-pointing unit normal vector $n.$\n\n*Hint: You're given that the fundamental vector product of $S$ with respect to its canonical parametrization is $||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||=\\sqrt{11}.$*" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=3x-y$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-3,\\,1,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=0,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨3,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=0.\n$$\n\nSince we require a downward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is negative. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨3,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨3,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}⟨3,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=3x-y$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨y,\\,x,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}⟨3,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}⟨y,\\,x,\\,3x-y⟩\\cdot ⟨3,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}(3y-x-(3x-y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}(4y-4x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}(4y-4x)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}(4y-4x)\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}(4y-4x)\\cdot \\sqrt{11}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{11}}}(4y-4x)\\cdot \\cancel{\\sqrt{11}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4y-4x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4y-4x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[4xy-2{x}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(4y-2)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[2{y}^{2}-2y]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1f385f05-45d4-59c1-9f32-a7029261c4c3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549078}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549109 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370846 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8eb87a35-5ced-5a5e-b4ba-6ed8f43fa65b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370848} {:answer/id #uuid "ed0b221a-37f0-59c2-9ebf-9e2be6f4943a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370849} {:answer/id #uuid "f449e0d2-71cb-570a-b7f8-b46a261c1889" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370850} {:answer/id #uuid "efe12eb7-f752-5e10-9617-78e76fdbdf10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370851} {:answer/id #uuid "574d87f2-9154-53de-97ba-85268e66a98b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370852}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370848} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ba53699-98f1-5f44-bc12-18afd01851f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370847}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "41a56a15-fd44-52b0-9c06-c58863771399" :question/math-academy-id "q-365881" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨y,\\,z,\\,2x-y⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=3$ for $1\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the *downward*-pointing unit normal vector $n.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is a continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor the surface $z=3$ for  $1\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 1,$ the downward-pointing unit normal vector is\n\n$$\nn=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=3$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨y,\\,z,\\,2x-y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨y,\\,3,\\,2x-y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-(2x-y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}(-(2x-y))\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(2x-y))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(-(2x-y))\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}[({x}^{2}-xy){|}_{x=1}^{x=2}\\,]\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}(3-y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{[3y-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "712af108-5684-50a0-a3e2-b8caf5dce7fc" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549080}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549130 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167129 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06b8703e-759f-5393-a228-e750d5de34f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{14}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275542} {:answer/id #uuid "6e25636d-cfd0-52ea-b503-2d5b025ef215" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{14}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276463} {:answer/id #uuid "85cb643e-79e3-5618-91fb-34cffdb30d3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276677} {:answer/id #uuid "d64fc3d4-8458-599c-b66c-82256044eeb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4\\sqrt{14}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277363} {:answer/id #uuid "df0dabc4-7904-5e22-a06d-f610075c3757" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277428}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277428} :answer-field/id #uuid "85b3546f-3513-5859-ad33-a711f16809ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277962}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "018ba806-567b-5332-8cf5-cb28969716b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-161158" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x,\\,y,\\,2z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=2x+3y$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$\n\n*Hint: You're given that the fundamental vector product of $S$ with respect to its canonical parametrization is $||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||=\\sqrt{14}.$*" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=2x+3y$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-2,\\,-3,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=0,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨2,\\,3,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=0.\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨-2,\\,-3,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-2,\\,-3,\\,1⟩}{\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}⟨-2,\\,-3,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2x+3y$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x,\\,y,\\,2z⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}⟨-2,\\,-3,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}⟨x,\\,y,\\,4x+6y⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,\\,-3,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}(-2x-3y+4x+6y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}(2x+3y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}(2x+3y)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}(2x+3y)\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}(2x+3y)\\cdot \\sqrt{14}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{14}}}(2x+3y)\\cdot \\cancel{\\sqrt{14}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x+3y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2x+3y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{x}^{2}+3xy]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}1+3y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[y+\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9e6a9636-664b-53c4-ba08-3b6f4a1ddd72" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549085}}] :activity/title "Flux in Three-Dimensional Vector Fields" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548345}]}
